<SiteLayout title="SRT vs VTT: Which Subtitle Format Should You Use? | ConvertAnyFile" description="Compare SRT and WebVTT for compatibility, web video, cue metadata, styling, timing syntax, and conversion. Learn when each subtitle format is the better choice.">
<section class="page-hero"><div class="site-container hero-inner"><div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/guides/">Guides</a><span>/</span><span>SRT vs VTT</span></div><span class="eyebrow">Subtitle formats</span><h1>SRT vs VTT: which subtitle format should you use?</h1><p>SRT is simple and broadly compatible. WebVTT is designed for web media and can carry richer browser-oriented metadata. The right format depends on the player, editor, publishing platform, and features you actually need.</p></div></section>
<ArticleFrame toc={toc} related={related} relatedTitle="Related subtitle tools & guides">
<div class="quick-answer" id="quick-answer"><strong>Quick answer</strong><p>Use <strong>SRT</strong> when broad compatibility and simplicity matter most. Use <strong>VTT</strong> when subtitles are intended for browser playback or you need WebVTT-specific cue settings and document metadata. If a destination only accepts one format, convert from the other and keep the original.</p></div>
<h2 id="differences">Key differences between SRT and VTT</h2><p>Both formats store timed text, but they use different syntax and capabilities. SRT typically consists of numbered cues with start/end timestamps and subtitle text. WebVTT begins with a <code>WEBVTT</code> header and can support cue identifiers, settings, NOTE blocks, STYLE blocks, REGION definitions, and other web-oriented metadata.</p><p>SRT timestamps use a comma before milliseconds, while WebVTT uses a period. That small syntax difference is enough to make simple extension renaming unreliable.</p>
<h2 id="when-srt">When SRT is the better choice</h2><ul><li>Your video editor, media player, or upload platform explicitly asks for SRT.</li><li>You want a simple, widely recognized subtitle exchange format.</li><li>You do not rely on advanced browser-specific positioning or styling.</li><li>You need an easy-to-inspect text format for basic caption timing.</li></ul>
<h2 id="when-vtt">When VTT is the better choice</h2><ul><li>The subtitles will be used with HTML5 video or a web player.</li><li>You need WebVTT cue settings, identifiers, regions, or STYLE metadata.</li><li>Your publishing workflow specifically requests <code>.vtt</code>.</li><li>You want a format designed around browser media behavior.</li></ul>
<h2 id="convert">What happens when you convert between SRT and VTT?</h2><p>Basic dialogue, cue order, and timing generally map well in either direction. The main fidelity risk appears when converting a richer VTT document into SRT because SRT cannot represent all WebVTT metadata.</p><p>For SRT to VTT, conversion mainly rewrites valid timed cues into WebVTT syntax. For VTT to SRT, the result should be considered a simpler compatibility copy if the original uses advanced metadata.</p><div class="callout"><strong>Do not replace the source immediately</strong><p>Keep the original subtitle until you have tested the converted file in its actual destination. Compatibility success matters more than the extension alone.</p></div>
<h2 id="faq">Common SRT vs VTT questions</h2><h3>Is VTT better than SRT?</h3><p>Not universally. VTT has richer web features, while SRT wins on simplicity and broad compatibility.</p><h3>Can browsers use SRT directly?</h3><p>Browser media workflows commonly expect WebVTT. A site or player may provide its own handling, but VTT is the safer native web choice.</p><h3>Can I convert without changing the dialogue?</h3><p>Yes. Format conversion does not translate the text. It rewrites supported timed-text structure for the destination format.</p><h3>Which is smaller?</h3><p>For ordinary subtitle files the size difference is usually insignificant. Compatibility and metadata support matter more.</p>
